WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:04.560
- Hawaii, California, Nevada,
Brazil, and Australia.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.550
These places have one very
flammable thing in common:

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:09.900
invasive grasses.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.060
These plants are fueling more frequent

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:15.090
and faster burning fires
that are harder to put out

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:17.850
and potentially more
catastrophic to human life.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.670
I'm gonna show you how and
what's being done to fix it.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:22.350
Grasses are considered invasive

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:25.440
when they take over new ecosystems
where they don't belong.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.390
Cheatgrass, which now blankets the basins

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:28.530
of the American West,

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:31.140
originally came from
either Europe or Eurasia.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.460
And guinea and buffelgrasses,

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.860
which likely played a big
role in the lahaina fire

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:35.850
are from Africa.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:38.820
Invasive grasses are among
the most ignitable fuels

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:39.750
for fires.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:40.680
- Look at the grasses.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:43.230
It's like a lot of gasoline on the ground.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.690
- Unlike the leaves of
trees or other plants,

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:47.580
grass doesn't decompose very quickly.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.470
It can stick around for a long time.

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:51.270
Because their leaves are so thin,

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:54.690
grasses also dry out fast,
making them extra flammable.

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:57.270
- If the air is hot and dry,

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:00.570
they will come to zero
moisture content, right?

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:03.570
And if you have a fire
coming down a mountain

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:06.592
or a power line falling into dry grass,

00:01:06.592 --> 00:01:08.730
it's gonna ignite instantly.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:09.990
- Just look back at places

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.930
where we've seen intense
grass-fed fires lately

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.750
or even the recent
fires in Canada, Greece,

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.130
and Washington State.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:19.350
Dry weather is a huge contributor,

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:20.880
and experts say dry seasons

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.030
are becoming longer and more
extreme due to climate change.

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.110
Under those conditions,

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:27.120
grasses can really show
how well they thrive

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.740
even in extreme environments.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:32.070
Their seeds can sprout
plants very, very quickly.

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:35.310
- You get a little bit of
rain, boom, they're off.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:37.920
- Their shallow roots also expand fast,

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:40.530
meaning they can grab
water from a large area,

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:43.290
often way faster than native plants.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:45.540
That puts native species
at a disadvantage.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.820
Add fire to the mix,
and that handicap grows

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:51.330
because invasive grasses
are very resilient to fire.

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:52.710
So after it all burns...

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:54.330
- All that comes back is grass.

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:56.430
- That's happening a
lot across the country.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:59.580
Without taller trees and shrubs
acting like windbreakers,

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:00.930
wind speeds go up.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:03.480
That fans fires and
helps them move faster,

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:04.950
making them harder to control.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:05.783
In other words,

00:02:05.783 --> 00:02:08.340
invasive grass is not only fuel fires,

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.350
they also make the
environments they invade

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:11.820
more fire-friendly.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:13.380
It's called the grass-fire cycle,

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:16.050
but human activity is
making grass-fueled fires

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:18.480
more problematic and potentially deadly.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.412
All of a sudden, we're
seeing a lot more fires

00:02:20.412 --> 00:02:22.560
in places where you wouldn't expect them,

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:23.700
like deserts.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:27.210
One big issue is land use
for housing and agriculture.

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:29.310
More people live in
close proximity to areas

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:30.360
that are fire-prone.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.310
- In California, there's a lot more people

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:35.280
moving into lowland areas.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.560
- [Daniela] On December 30th, 2021,

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:40.080
the Marshall Fire started
near Boulder, Colorado.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.290
- It was a fire that started in dry grass

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:46.620
that was completely surrounding
a housing development,

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:48.990
very similar to the Maui situation

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:50.790
that it didn't have to go very far

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:52.800
before it hit the first house.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.890
- 1,084 houses went up in flames.

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:58.500
The Marshall Fire got
started from burning trash

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.540
and a power line that emit sparks.

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:03.690
In Maui, abandoned pineapple
and sugarcane fields

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:05.790
filled up with guinea and buffelgrasses.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:08.370
These invasive grasses
then likely fueled the fire

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:09.720
that destroyed Lahaina.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:12.300
Another important variable is emissions.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:13.470
All over the US,

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:16.770
modern vehicles with combustion
engines spread out ammonia,

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:18.930
which is the nitrogen
source that grasses love.

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:19.950
It's the building block

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:22.110
of nitrogen-based commercial fertilizers.

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:24.600
- We're creating a really nice environment

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:25.920
for them to grow in.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:28.110
- So, does this mean
that devastating fires

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:30.120
like the ones in Hawaii and Colorado

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:31.740
are going to become more common?

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:36.570
- It's a preview of what happens
when you are not prepared

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:39.990
and when you are not
planning for the extreme.

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:42.900
- Things like rebuilding roads
to make evacuations easier

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:45.060
and retrofitting houses
to make them resilient

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:46.830
in a more flammable world.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:49.350
Other survival strategies
include controlled burns

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:50.880
of grass-invaded areas

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:53.580
and then planting
less-flammable vegetation.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:55.890
Community preparedness is also critical.

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:58.440
- We need to imagine
our worst-case scenario

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:00.237
and then say, "Okay, are we ready?"

